::: {.doc-subject}

Calculate reciprocity (fraction of edges with reciprocal counterpart).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L92208-L92328){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.reciprocity(mode="default")
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Reciprocity](../assets/graphs/reciprocity.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A five-node directed graph with seven arcs. The two reciprocal pairs, A and B and C and D, are drawn in the accent color: four of the seven arcs have a partner pointing back, so reciprocity is 4 / 7 = 0.571." width="100%"}

Reciprocity measures the proportion of edges in a directed graph that are bidirectional
(mutual). Returns a value between `0.0` (no reciprocal edges) and `1.0` (all edges are
reciprocal).

This metric is fundamental for understanding relationship symmetry in social networks,
mutual influence in citation networks, and bidirectional flows in transportation or
communication networks.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[mode]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;default&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   How reciprocity is counted. With `"default"`, it is the share of edges whose reverse
    edge also exists, as in NetworkX. With `"ratio"`, it is the share of connected pairs
    of nodes that are joined both ways, as in igraph: each pair counts once, whether it
    is joined in one direction or both. A graph whose share of reciprocated edges is
    `r` has a ratio of `r / (2 - r)` (when it has no self-loops or parallel edges).
    Self-loops are ignored by `"ratio"`, and by both modes when finding mutual pairs.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Reciprocity value between `0.0` and `1.0`, or `0.0` for empty graphs.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If called on an undirected graph or with invalid mode.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~which_mutual`) 

:   List the mutual pairs of edges.

[](`~count_mutual_node_pairs`) 

:   Count the pairs of nodes joined both ways.

[](`~triadic_census`) 

:   Count the patterns of edges among three nodes.

[](`~density`) 

:   The value that reciprocity would have by chance.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Of the three edges, two form a mutual pair, so the reciprocity is `2 / 3`. Counted by
pairs of nodes instead, one of the two connected pairs is mutual:

::: {#ad23b675 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("Ana", "Ben"), ("Ben", "Ana"), ("Ana", "Cruz")], directed=True
)

round(graph.reciprocity(), 3), graph.reciprocity(mode="ratio")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(0.667, 0.5)
```
:::
:::


The mutual pair picked out. The edge from Ana to Cruz has no edge back:

::: {#ee118b1f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def mutual_edges(g):
    edges = g.get_edge_df()
    pairs = set(zip(edges["source"], edges["target"]))
    return [
        e
        for e, s, t in zip(edges["id"], edges["source"], edges["target"])
        if (t, s) in pairs
    ]

graph.plot(
    highlight_edges=mutual_edges(graph), label_by="label",
    curved_edges=True, height=240, seed=1,
)
```

